his artworks are big colorful and  eye-catching but a closer look shows  something more they're made of trash  Tomas tambo is a designer and artist who  proves that one man's trash is another  man's treasure all his sculptures and  projects are made of things that have  been thrown away I've been using  recycled materials my whole life and as  I grew older I still wanted to build a  lot of stuff and be creative and like  this but I still didn't have a lot of  money and I learned at that point that  it made no sense to go and buy it when I  could just find it Tomas sorts through  his latest acquisitions tense yoga mats  and sundry items left behind at a music  festival he's got plans for them in his  next project we're gonna make workshops  where people can make hats and like a  superhero masks out of the mess now when  I'm standing and and sorting it out also  get a little idea of okay maybe I can  put this one together with this one and  it can become this one first he goes  scavenging then he develops the project  to suit what he's found that's also how  he came up with the happy wall the wall  is made up of almost 2,000 analog pixels  made of bits of wood left over from the  roskilde festival stage dambo  constructed the interactive wall in  central Copenhagen in 2013 the idea  behind heavy wall is to allow people to  express themselves in in a big and  powerful way in a public space  passers-by were invited to photograph  their messages and post them on social  media with the hashtag happy wall over  7,000 photos of the happy wall were  uploaded in just one year if you write  something that the the general public  won't like then the general public will  just go and they'll flip the board and  so in this way it's in it's a democratic  way to chew  what you can see in the city and unlike  commercials which are defined by who has  the money and the means the 35 year old  artist is also keen for the public to  interact with his current installation  it's street art with a difference 52  bird houses made of recycled wood are  part of an open-air exhibition at the  Arkin museum of modern art that is if  they can be found well we chose Thomas  Dumbo to be part of this exhibition  because he is very much in accordance  with the way that we think at the museum  we think that the museum is there for  the public and Thomas Tampa really  employs the public he involves the  public and he communicates with the  level of the public so to speak and that  has really engaged the public a lot the  artist has installed some 3,500 bird  houses since he started building them in  2007 he's taken his recycling message  from Copenhagen as far as Lebanon this  street art is for the birds and  open-minded viewers since one of my  friends has been in jail for painting  graffiti I was inspired to do some kind  of a project but and when I put it up or  when I painted it or when i sticked it  at the wall or how we normally do in the  street art project and the people will  not like call the police but they would  instill instead call me and say thank  you his next project is also designed to  give people a positive experience with  recycling for an upcoming festival he's  planning a sculpture of a mountain with  a flowing water fall it'll be made of  6,000 wooden triangles he's found hidden  behind the waterfall will be a space for  meetings workshops and dancing where we  are recycling right now at like the  level we are as a world with real like a  beginner level you know we are really  really bad at it and I hope that my work  and can help us just come up a little  bit you know and and I hope that I also  will learn to get better they're true  doing it in  hopefully I have a lot of years left  where I can do like bigger and bigger  project because I have a lot of ideas  left Tomas Tomas fantastical creations  give new life to old things and he makes  people think twice about what they toss  out
 

